wait, slot 1 was saved at sHit3 state which was after play_input while paused? save_state slot 1 was done while paused (paused:true). So it should restore to 2 hearts, enemy screen. But slot 1 was saved BEFORE I did the writes and heart tests, at sHit3. At that time health was 2 hearts. Good.\n\nHmm, but actually the heart display at sHit3 might have been 2 hearts (2.0) and health... whatever.\n\nLet me load slot 1, pause, read 0x02F0-0x0350, and screenshot.\n\nActually, I realize a cleaner path now: The HUD buffer at 0x0312 holds the heart tile. There should be similar bytes for heart1, heart2, heart3, and the counters. If I find the health variable, the HUD routine reads it. \n\nLet me look at the buffer. Let me read 0x0300 length 64 now (current state). And screenshot to know the state.\n\nHmm, let me first re-establish a known state. Let me reload the clean state and pause, then read the HUD buffer at 3 hearts. Then get to 2 hearts (I can do the damage dance again or load slot 1) and read again.\n\nActually, here's a thought: maybe I can find health by WRITING to the HUD buffer? No, that's output.\n\nLet me try the direct approach: load slot 1 (2 hearts), then write a value to each of the dD candidates and see which changes hearts. But to do 52 tests, I'd batch: I can write to multiple candidates in one message (multiple write_memory calls), then step and screenshot. If hearts change, narrow. With 52 candidates, binary search: write a distinctive value (say 6) to half of them (26 bytes, but they're not contiguous so 26 separate write_memory calls), step, screenshot. If hearts change → health in that half. But writing 6 to 26 random RAM bytes at once may crash the game; and the HUD might not update if the game overwrites.\n\nRisky but save state protects.\n\nHmm, this is getting very long. Let me reconsider whether there's a smarter way.\n\nAH WAIT. I just realized: maybe health is stored as a 16-bit value and the HUD routine reads it, and the \"changed\" bytes 0