{"type":"thinking","text":"Hmm, \"no instructions decoded\" for 0xEEA0. Maybe that bank isn't currently mapped, or the disassembler fails because the CPU can't read that address right now (bank switched). The disassemble tool reads CPU memory; if bank 0xE000 currently maps to a different ROM bank, the bytes differ. But it should still decode something. Unless the read returned zeros.\n\nLet me use read_memory mem_type cpu at 0xEEA0 to see bytes. Or disassemble 0xEED0.\n\nActually, the earlier successful disassembly at 0xED08 worked. Maybe 0xEEA0 is in a different bank not currently mapped. Let me disassemble 0xEEDE again but maybe the address 0xEEDE is not code and the disassembler bailed after failing? \"no instructions decoded\" is odd.\n\nLet me just disassemble 0xED08 further (I got 48 bytes; let me get more) — the routine around 0xED08 reads 0x066F and calls various routines. But the HUD shadow writer was 0xEEDE.\n\nHmm, let me disassemble 0xEE00-0xEF00 in pieces. Let me try address 0xEE00 = 60928 length 128.\n\nActually, maybe the issue is that disassembler requires aligned start. Let me try 0xEED0.\n\nLet me do a few: 0xEED0, 0xEE80.\n\nHmm, I'm burning calls. Let me instead read the raw bytes at CPU 0xEE00-0xEF00 and disassemble mentally? No.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe I don't need this. Let me go back to the differential and just brute-force test the candidates.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole approach for health using a cleaner differential: instead of getting hit, let me use the HUD shadow. Since the HUD shadow at 0x0310 = 242 (full heart) changed to 102 (empty) when damaged... wait, dD showed 0x0312: 242->102. And the trace showed writes to 0x310. Hmm.\n\nLet me trace READS of the health... no.\n\nNew idea: The game's \"heart\" display uses full heart tile 242, empty heart tile 102 (and probably half heart tile 226 or similar). The routine that decides which tile to draw reads the health. If I can find the health byte by looking at RAM values that correspond to the heart state...\n\nOK, let me just do the brute-force write test but smartly. I'll take the candidate set = bytes that changed between full (A0) and 2 hearts (D0), excluding sprite/OAM (0x0200-0x02FF). From the full dD list, that's maybe 20 candidates. Let me test each by writing a value that should visibly change hearts, e.g., write 6 (full) when at 2 hearts, and see if hearts become full. Or write 0 (empty).\n\nBut I need to be at a known damaged state. Let me get to 2 hearts. I can load slot 1 (2 hearts). Then for each candidate, write 6, step 2 frames, screenshot, and check heart pixels; then reload slot 1. That's ~20 iterations, too slow.\n\nBetter: write 6 to ALL candidates at once, step, screenshot. If hearts become full → health is among them. Then split. Writing to ~20 candidates at once might crash, but save state protects. Actually the candidates include OAM shadow if I don't exclude; let me exclude 0x200-0x2FF.\n\nCandidates (non-OAM) from dD:\n0x0005, 0x000F, 0x0067, 0x0074, 0x007A, 0x0084, 0x008E, 0x009C, 0x00A2, 0x01F0, 0x0312, 0x03FC, 0x043A, 0x049E, 0x05F2, 0x0608, 0x0610, 0x066F, 0x0670.\nPlus 0x0248,0x024C (OAM) excluded.\n\n0x0312 is the HUD shadow (writing it might change the heart tile directly!). Hmm, if I write 6 to 0x0312, it'd set the tile shadow to 6 (garbage tile). That would change the heart display but not via health. So 0x0312 could give a false positive. Let me exclude 0x0312 too (it's an output, not health).\n\nActually, wait — maybe health is NOT in dD because it didn't satisfy dA (unchanged during idle). Let me instead compute a better candidate set: bytes that changed between B0 and D0 AND between A0 and C0 (both damage events). Health satisfies both. Let me do:\n- dE = diff A0,C0 filter changed (no from)\n- dF = diff B0,D0 filter changed from dE\nThat intersection = bytes changed by both damage events. Health is in there (and so are position/animation bytes). Then test.\n\nHmm, still many.\n\nAlternatively, bytes that DECREASE by the same delta on each half-heart:\n- dG = diff A0,C0 filter decreased\n- dH = diff C0,D0 filter decreased from dG\nHealth, if it decreases, would be in dH. Earlier dB/dC used B0/C0/D0 and found 7 survivors none health. Let me instead try without the dA filter (since dA might exclude health). \n\nLet me do: dG = diff A0,C0 decreased (all), dH = diff C0,D0 decreased from dG. Then look for health.\n\nBut if health is stored as a decreasing byte, it should survive. Earlier with dA pre-filter, dB gave 44 and dC gave 7, none health. Without dA, the sets are larger but health should still survive (health decreased on both). Wait, health decreasing: at A0 full (6?), C0 2.5 (5?), D0 2.0 (4?). So health decreased A0→C0 and C0→D0. It should be in dG and dH. In dB/dC it wasn't, but dB/dC required health unchanged during idle (dA) — and health IS unchanged during idle, so it should've been included... unless health is NOT a simple decreasing byte.\n\nSince health wasn't in dC (7 survivors), health must NOT be a byte that simply decreased on both hits. So either it increases, or it changes by a non-decrease, or it's a multi-byte/bitfield.\n\nLet me check the INCREASED direction:\n- dI = diff A0,C0 increased\n- dJ = diff C0,D0 increased from dI\nFind a byte that increases on both hits. Could be a \"damage counter\".\n\nLet me run these. Actually, let me run dI with from dA to exclude timers.\n\nHmm, but if health is a bitfield where bits are cleared, the byte value decreases. Hmm.\n\nLet me just run: dI = diff A0,C0 filter changed from dA (all changed, excluding timers). Then dJ = diff C0,D0 filter changed from dI. This gives bytes changed by BOTH damage events (excluding timers) = health + position + animation. Then I look for the health one.\n\nWait, I already have dD = B0,D0 changed from dA. Let me instead do dI = A0,C0 changed from dA, then dJ = C0,D0 changed from dI. Health should be in dJ. Let me do it.\n\nThen among dJ, look for a byte whose values are consistent with hearts. Let me run.\n\nActually, position bytes also change on both. But there should be few total.\n\nLet me run dI and dJ.\n\nWe're currently at... after the 600-frame trace, the game state is unknown (Link may be dead or on enemy screen). Snapshots A0/B0/C0/D0 are stored, so diffs still work regardless of current state. Good.\n\nLet me run:\n1. diff A0 C0 filter changed from dA name dI\n2. diff C0 D0 filter changed from dI name dJ\n\nLet me do both in one message? They're dependent (dJ needs dI), so sequential.\n\nLet me run dI first."}
